WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:01.801
-I'm looking for --
-Oh!

00:00:01.801 --> 00:00:02.801
♪ Good day, miss ♪

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:04.434
♪ Lookin' pretty,
lookin' lonely ♪

00:00:04.434 --> 00:00:06.067
♪ Looks like you could
use a child ♪

00:00:06.067 --> 00:00:08.234
♪ Or two or three ♪

00:00:08.234 --> 00:00:09.767
♪ For a very modest fee ♪

00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:11.734
♪ If, by any chance,
you're searchin' ♪

00:00:11.734 --> 00:00:13.000
♪ For an urchin ♪

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.567
♪ I got your merchan...dise
right here ♪

00:00:15.567 --> 00:00:17.734
♪ But I'll be clear,
tell you straight ♪

00:00:17.734 --> 00:00:19.000
♪ They're not great ♪

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.601
♪ And they don't appreciate ♪

00:00:20.601 --> 00:00:24.434
♪ They're possibly
the worst brats in town ♪

00:00:24.434 --> 00:00:26.200
-So, you're s...elling
the orphans?

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.334
-♪ And me? ♪

00:00:28.334 --> 00:00:31.133
♪ I'm just a woman
with needs of me own ♪

00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:33.334
♪ Who'd like to do
some misdeeds of me own ♪

00:00:33.334 --> 00:00:36.934
[ Laughs ]
♪ With a certain someone ♪

00:00:36.934 --> 00:00:38.234
[ Cheering and applause ]
-Hey, welcome back.

00:00:38.234 --> 00:00:40.734
We're here at "Late Night"
with Kristin Chenoweth.

00:00:40.734 --> 00:00:43.267
That was a clip
from "Schmigadoon!"

00:00:43.267 --> 00:00:46.400
Before we get to "Schmigadoon!,"
I'm going to say, real quickly,

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.667
we're coming up on the
20th Anniversary of "Wicked."

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:50.234
Can you believe it?
-No.

00:00:50.234 --> 00:00:51.767
[ Cheering and applause ]
No.

00:00:51.767 --> 00:00:53.968
-This is a role,

00:00:53.968 --> 00:00:56.767
obviously, a lot of people
still associate you with.

00:00:56.767 --> 00:00:58.501
I mean, they associate you
with a lot of different things,

00:00:58.501 --> 00:00:59.901
but we were talking backstage.

00:00:59.901 --> 00:01:02.434
You only you only did "Wicked"
for nine months.

00:01:02.434 --> 00:01:03.667
-Nine months.

00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:06.267
-That is crazy.
-In fact, like five years ago,

00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:09.634
I was in CVS
on like 73rd and Broadway,

00:01:09.634 --> 00:01:11.901
and I was just doing
my own thing at 7:30,

00:01:11.901 --> 00:01:13.300
getting all my crap.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.701
[ Laughter ]
And the lady comes in
and she goes,

00:01:15.701 --> 00:01:17.267
"You're -- It's half hour.

00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:18.200
You need to get to the theater."

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:21.400
[ Laughter ]

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:24.100
I said, "Oh, I'm not in a show
right now."

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:25.334
She goes, "Yes, you are.
You've got 'Wicked.'

00:01:25.334 --> 00:01:26.834
You need to be
at the Gershwin, now."

00:01:26.834 --> 00:01:30.167
[ Laughter ]
I was like, "Don't argue
with me, lady. I know me.

00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:32.300
[ Laughter ]
It's been 15 years."
And now, it's 20.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:33.400
I can't believe it.
-I can't believe it, either.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.234
-And I was only 15
when I was cast.

00:01:35.234 --> 00:01:36.868
[ Cheering and applause ]
-Oh, my God.
-Kidding.

00:01:36.868 --> 00:01:38.000
-So young.
-♪ I'm kidding ♪

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.100
-Yes. So precocious at the time.
-Oh, yes.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.534
-This is such a special show.

00:01:42.534 --> 00:01:43.667
It's such a special cast.

00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:45.434
But it's, you know,
it's inspired

00:01:45.434 --> 00:01:47.067
by the musical -- this season,

00:01:47.067 --> 00:01:48.634
by the musicals
of the '60s and '70s.

00:01:48.634 --> 00:01:50.968
But it's like
a musical variety show

00:01:50.968 --> 00:01:52.968
and there's nothing like it.

00:01:52.968 --> 00:01:54.868
I mean, it feels like
from a bygone era.

00:01:54.868 --> 00:01:57.334
-It is.
And, Seth, for me,

00:01:57.334 --> 00:02:01.400
for my comrades --
I call them comrades.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.133
You know, it's Alan
and Jane Krakowski

00:02:04.133 --> 00:02:10.667
and DuBose and Dove and Jaime
and Ann Harada and all of us.

00:02:10.667 --> 00:02:13.100
It feels like
our "Saturday Night Live."

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:16.033
Like it feels like
a Christopher Guest thing

00:02:16.033 --> 00:02:17.067
that put us together.

00:02:17.067 --> 00:02:19.234
We're allowed to do
our music theater

00:02:19.234 --> 00:02:23.200
and sort of make fun of
and also love our milieu.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.868
And, for me, it's the best job.

00:02:25.868 --> 00:02:28.234
And I just --
Cinco Paul's brain --

00:02:28.234 --> 00:02:30.167
our writer -- it must hurt.

00:02:30.167 --> 00:02:32.200
[ Laughter ]
There's a lot of noodles
in there.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:33.501
And this year he gave --

00:02:33.501 --> 00:02:35.200
you know, last year,
I played a very buttoned up --

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:36.267
I had a --

00:02:36.267 --> 00:02:38.734
I had a five-page song

00:02:38.734 --> 00:02:40.400
[ Cheering and applause ]
without a cut.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.501
-Yeah. A very fast...
-Patter.

00:02:42.501 --> 00:02:44.534
-...yeah, patter song.
-I mean, talk about sweat,

00:02:44.534 --> 00:02:46.300
you know? This year
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:48.934
the role is a hybrid

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:50.701
of Miss Hannigan from "Annie"

00:02:50.701 --> 00:02:52.300
and Miss Lovett
from "Sweeney Todd."

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:53.801
-I mean, those are
some pretty meaty roles.

00:02:53.801 --> 00:02:56.200
-They're very meaty.
And the singing --

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:57.567
he always gives me hard stuff.

00:02:57.567 --> 00:02:58.601
I'm like, "Really?"

00:02:58.601 --> 00:03:00.767
[ Laughter ]
But, actually, I love it.

00:03:00.767 --> 00:03:05.400
It's a challenge and everybody
just knocks it out of the park.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.400
We think that we are very lucky

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:10.434
to be doing what we love to do
on camera.

00:03:10.434 --> 00:03:12.601
-It's really fun.
Anytime you see

00:03:12.601 --> 00:03:14.567
that the people making the show
love it

00:03:14.567 --> 00:03:16.400
and there are so many people,
obviously,

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.734
who love that world
on a granular level,

00:03:18.734 --> 00:03:21.067
who can appreciate exactly
what the references are.

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:22.734
And then there's people like me,
who just appreciate it

00:03:22.734 --> 00:03:23.901
for a great performance,

00:03:23.901 --> 00:03:25.267
which is really fun to watch.
-Thank you.

00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:27.234
That would be like my brother,
who said, "Hey, listen,

00:03:27.234 --> 00:03:29.167
don't take me to any more
musical theater, hey.

00:03:29.167 --> 00:03:30.601
I love ya.
I don't like it."

00:03:30.601 --> 00:03:32.501
[ Laughter ]
But he loves the show

00:03:32.501 --> 00:03:35.801
because Keegan and Cecily
are like hilarious

00:03:35.801 --> 00:03:38.901
and then we're all wacko
around them, so.

00:03:38.901 --> 00:03:42.234
[ Laughter ]
-You know what I think it shows?

00:03:42.234 --> 00:03:44.801
Which is, even people
who do musical theater

00:03:44.801 --> 00:03:47.501
appreciate that there are
some eyerolly tropes

00:03:47.501 --> 00:03:49.234
about musical theater.
-We all can.

00:03:49.234 --> 00:03:51.400
-Right.
And so this show both has

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.334
the execution of wonderful songs

00:03:53.334 --> 00:03:54.767
that you would see
in a musical theater

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:57.267
and the characters who are
standing on the outside of it,

00:03:57.267 --> 00:03:59.234
who maybe have
your brother's perspective,

00:03:59.234 --> 00:04:01.200
which is why people
from both sides can come in

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:02.567
and enjoy the show.
-That's exactly right.

00:04:02.567 --> 00:04:04.901
I mean, when he saw last season,
"You know what?

00:04:04.901 --> 00:04:07.734
I like, you know,
I like the show."

00:04:07.734 --> 00:04:09.434
[ Laughter ]
I'm like, "That's a huge

00:04:09.434 --> 00:04:11.000
compliment, for you, isn't it?
Thank you."

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:12.567
[ Laughter ]

00:04:12.567 --> 00:04:14.133
Alan -- we've talked about your
friendship with Alan Cummings.

00:04:14.133 --> 00:04:15.367
How many years are you guys
going on,

00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:17.234
as far as not just friends,
but collaborators?

00:04:17.234 --> 00:04:20.567
-I want to say 26 or 27.

00:04:20.567 --> 00:04:22.234
He knows me and I
know him real well.

00:04:22.234 --> 00:04:24.367
In fact, I was just
on a cruise.

00:04:24.367 --> 00:04:26.167
While it was at dock
because I don't cruise

00:04:26.167 --> 00:04:27.734
because I have veneers.
It doesn't matter.

00:04:27.734 --> 00:04:31.200
[ Laughter ]
And I can't -- like I can't --
I have vertigo.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.000
And I was headlining this thing
and he was singing as well

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.801
and we just laugh and have fun.

00:04:36.801 --> 00:04:38.834
And I think we should
all count ourselves lucky

00:04:38.834 --> 00:04:41.801
if we have one friend
that lasts that long,

00:04:41.801 --> 00:04:44.634
that you work with,
that you can laugh, still.

00:04:44.634 --> 00:04:47.334
I mean, we still think
we got it.

00:04:47.334 --> 00:04:48.467
We still think we got it.

00:04:48.467 --> 00:04:50.000
-Well, we think so as well.
-Thank you.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:52.534
-Somebody else
who still has it,

00:04:52.534 --> 00:04:54.567
which is incredible
and worth honoring,

00:04:54.567 --> 00:04:56.934
and you did just honor,
is Carol Burnett.

00:04:56.934 --> 00:04:59.734
[ Cheering and applause ]
It was a special event
for Carol Burnett.

00:04:59.734 --> 00:05:01.901
I had to double-check it.
Is it true?

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:04.601
Is 90 the number?

00:05:04.601 --> 00:05:07.300
-She's 90.
-That is incredible!

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.734
Y'all, she is so much the same.

00:05:09.734 --> 00:05:11.133
She is so
[ Snapping fingers ] sharp.

00:05:11.133 --> 00:05:13.200
She's been a mentor of mine
for several years.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.067
I might not want to say like 20.

00:05:15.067 --> 00:05:16.334
And when we get together,

00:05:16.334 --> 00:05:17.701
it's usually not
about show business.

00:05:17.701 --> 00:05:19.968
It's usually about romance
and life and all that stuff.

00:05:19.968 --> 00:05:23.033
And she's been there
through all the guys.

00:05:23.033 --> 00:05:24.567
[ Laughter and ohhs ]
I shouldn't --

00:05:24.567 --> 00:05:25.934
[ Applause ]
Oops!

00:05:25.934 --> 00:05:27.968
-Most of those are
from Vancouver, right? Yeah.

00:05:27.968 --> 00:05:29.133
[ Laughter ]
-Of course!

00:05:29.133 --> 00:05:30.734
Where I left them.

00:05:30.734 --> 00:05:33.234
[ Laughter ]
So, she has been
a great mentor to me

00:05:33.234 --> 00:05:36.834
and she's accepted and loved me
and given me great advice.

00:05:36.834 --> 00:05:39.367
But we did a tribute
to her 90th birthday

00:05:39.367 --> 00:05:40.634
and all the years
in television.

00:05:40.634 --> 00:05:42.033
And think about this --

00:05:42.033 --> 00:05:44.601
so, I love her,
Bernadette Peters,

00:05:44.601 --> 00:05:45.634
and Julie Andrews.
Those are my

00:05:45.634 --> 00:05:47.400
music theater queens, right?
-Whoo!

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.968
-Here she is sitting
by her bestie, Julie,

00:05:49.968 --> 00:05:51.901
and then I'm singing
with Bernadette.

00:05:51.901 --> 00:05:54.000
If you want to talk
about a Mylanta moment,

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.400
[ Laughter ]
that was one.

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:58.200
-Now, can I ask?
In a moment like that,

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.133
can you be in the moment
and enjoy it

00:06:00.133 --> 00:06:02.400
or are you just so terrified?

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.767
Because I feel I have
to remind myself --

00:06:04.767 --> 00:06:06.067
not that I've had a moment
quite like that --

00:06:06.067 --> 00:06:08.000
but were are you like,
"Oh, this will only --

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:09.601
I will only be able
to look back on it fondly

00:06:09.601 --> 00:06:10.868
if it goes well"?

00:06:10.868 --> 00:06:13.033
And you seem, so effortlessly,
to pull those off.

00:06:13.033 --> 00:06:14.601
But do you have butterflies?

00:06:14.601 --> 00:06:15.767
-Of course.

00:06:15.767 --> 00:06:17.601
Not necessarily with Carol
so much, anymore,

00:06:17.601 --> 00:06:19.000
because we have spent
so much time

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:21.901
and we both like Cosmos
and anyway.

00:06:21.901 --> 00:06:23.601
[ Laughter ]
We've spent a lot.

00:06:23.601 --> 00:06:26.067
But still Julie.
I mean --

00:06:26.067 --> 00:06:27.667
[ As Andrews ]
♪ The hills are alive ♪

00:06:27.667 --> 00:06:30.667
[ Cheering and applause ]
That's why I became a singer!

00:06:30.667 --> 00:06:32.033
-[ Laughs ]
-I love. I mean,

00:06:32.033 --> 00:06:33.334
that's why I became a singer.

00:06:33.334 --> 00:06:34.434
And then there's Bernadette.

00:06:34.434 --> 00:06:37.067
So, I just always say,
"You know what?

00:06:37.067 --> 00:06:39.601
You're of an age, now, that you

00:06:39.601 --> 00:06:40.868
are being invited to the table

00:06:40.868 --> 00:06:42.067
to do what you do
with the greats

00:06:42.067 --> 00:06:44.267
and just try to be
as great as you can."

00:06:44.267 --> 00:06:45.400
-Well, there you go.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:46.467
It's certainly working out,
so far.

00:06:46.467 --> 00:06:47.934
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:06:47.934 --> 00:06:50.601
-Thanks for having me.
-Kristin Chenoweth.

00:06:50.601 --> 00:06:51.868
[ Cheering and applause ]
New episodes of "Schmigadoon!"

00:06:51.868 --> 00:06:53.767
are available Wednesdays
on Apple TV+.

00:06:53.767 --> 00:06:56.133
"Carol Burnett:
90 Years of Laughter and Love"

00:06:56.133 --> 00:06:58.267
premieres April 26th here
on NBC.

00:06:58.267 --> 00:07:00.234
You can see Kristin there
as well.

00:07:00.234 --> 00:07:01.434
It's available the next day
on Peacock.

00:07:01.434 --> 00:07:02.968
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

